#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105610 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92b54d77-fce2-53bf-aaad-4c7db8285162" :knowledge-point/title "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the Y-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "fb897f41-dd8e-561f-be0e-02d2f9972203" :question/math-academy-id "e-5393" :question/problem "Plot the curve defined by the equation $r=4\\sin\\theta$ in polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "The polar equation $r=2a\\sin\\theta$ defines a circle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(0,a).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=4\\sin\\theta,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=4\\sin\\theta$ gives a circle of radius $\\left|2\\right| = 2$ centered at the point $(0,2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6a1f74e065f3508c92f8a438eb1830aeb4f877c355cd7779e86183a0da92183.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22b3da96-2573-513e-ae70-2b49510acd87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "9958848d-a3ec-599e-bbc4-44b3993bc707" :question/math-academy-id "e-5394" :question/problem "What is the polar equation of the curve shown below?\n\n![](images/0a/0ad44bfa452da482fff5f579f1be9569670f7ad8da347314bcb63f618eaf3d2a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a circle of radius $|a| = |-4.5|=4.5$ centered at the point $(0,-4.5).$ \n\nIn polar coordinates, the equation of such a circle is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2a\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-4.5)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105613 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ad4cf0e-4346-5277-9026-122b5ae58f25" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given a Point on the Circumference"}] :question/id #uuid "7e0646a3-f4d2-5a29-ac05-519494a06177" :question/math-academy-id "e-5396" :question/problem "The polar curve $r=k\\cos\\theta,$ where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $\\left(-\\dfrac72,\\dfrac72\\right).$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|-\\frac{(\\frac{7}{2})}{(\\frac{7}{2})}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $\\left(r, \\theta\\right)=\\left(\\dfrac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right).$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =k(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ k & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-7\\cos\\theta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8a/8aec18033a85129c44765d2670ebe9c931b0c0186c46b74f9b0ea0ae14028b76.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105669 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3504bc1-05d2-55c5-b862-84be2e2516f7" :knowledge-point/title "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the X-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "dd2bf9b1-9198-5040-9ef4-9924b4181108" :question/math-academy-id "e-7598" :question/problem "Plot the curve defined by the equation $r=2\\cos\\theta$ in polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "The polar equation $r=2a\\cos\\theta$ defines a circle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=2\\cos\\theta,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{2}{2} = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=2\\cos\\theta$ gives a circle of radius $\\left|1\\right| = 1$ centered at the point $(1,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/bc/bc5f763522aec68e97bef90126866fe6b2fe7246560d4dc9ddd2c71a5a524d88.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92b54d77-fce2-53bf-aaad-4c7db8285162" :knowledge-point/title "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f850ed-6f12-5ba5-94d5-18567bdc4616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b4274853168579b84e78d0088e5ffb2f9860ab16081f396abe775b3f1fe70ec0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542704} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9c344c-61e0-59de-859d-e58fe8743646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bd/bd1ae74e7aebb5dc30000cbc633a54ecc1bbae93f38ba4c1992bf98f51d2e86b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542812} {:answer/id #uuid "49883a17-6fa9-515a-b452-2a2496a65ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/174fe14d478316ed047722acaacbccd0a81b81e59c6fd4fa59fd2f8243345e91.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542842} {:answer/id #uuid "94b7f4c5-c4a8-589e-b74e-b5666ce51ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/18/182bed612e0b711da7ae1f024e5d0ea39a6766bbcda87a31a59d463bff79b765.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543028} {:answer/id #uuid "d0312b61-e5a8-5968-956b-736d5c37d363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2f/2fd520ba40e90549450b1296f4e9253bffb7a2b7ab397ccee1c9491d4f5235