4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar equation $r=2a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ defines a circle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na=\\frac{(-4)}{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ gives a circle of radius $|-2|=2$ centered at the point $(-2,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/94/946014c799034b7175481491be12ecf39315496de246bae366ef0ffa6ee7ec6c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ad4cf0e-4346-5277-9026-122b5ae58f25" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given a Point on the Circumference"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "981d3983-800a-55ea-b1a2-87bb9a5923a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102331} {:answer/id #uuid "f31f0539-6c19-5582-8910-def8c7b74c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102332} {:answer/id #uuid "51dfeb23-4fda-5db0-bbf7-d2f11386e580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102333} {:answer/id #uuid "56c36efd-e983-567d-9403-e137b209d5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102334} {:answer/id #uuid "5a76b867-a0f7-5b70-af29-345e081b2cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a76b867-a0f7-5b70-af29-345e081b2cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102335} :answer-field/id #uuid "e65acae8-473c-5fa7-a708-444204561900" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86e1c85f-e8f2-5108-a533-e87dddde9479" :question/math-academy-id "q-16477" :question/problem "The polar curve $r=k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(-1,1).$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|-\\frac{1}{1}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =k(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e46b6d286319331da310aecbaaf6b0aaa38243d239697e6c07374341e0ff62d8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92b54d77-fce2-53bf-aaad-4c7db8285162" :knowledge-point/title "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08031ef6-af65-5111-b382-2b6b89af3c2a" :an