cle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(0,a).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na=\\frac{(-7)}{2}=-\\frac{7}{2}=-3.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ gives a circle of radius $|-3.5|=3.5$ centered at the point $(0,-3.5),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/1e/1ec8b2c39de3bbb0bf91cf5fd3c075e266e12c21b625171111576258825eeb48.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22b3da96-2573-513e-ae70-2b49510acd87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bafe0ed7-0bd4-55e8-a804-d4e574db739d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bafe0ed7-0bd4-55e8-a804-d4e574db739d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543131} :answer-field/id #uuid "e203115c-e10d-5845-891b-2d11e9765a9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d345ebc0-fb4a-55bd-95ea-a1eddcced6e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-281480" :question/problem "![](images/49/49238b18533c2ded8180a8532325f14c3b052fb32c58a81892877fba2a4ee054.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the given curve is $r=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a circle of radius $|a|=|-6|=6$ centered at the point $(-6,0).$ \n\nIn polar coordinates, the equation of such a circle is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-6)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22b3da96-2573-513e-ae70-2b49510acd87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf10e9f-c6d6-5e2f-b906-1ed89575ede3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cf10e9f-c6d6-5e2f-b906-1ed89575ede3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542668} :answer-field/id #uuid "9954e5e0-82ec-58d8-b662-5f315b96877d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e14b9dd2-f663-55f7-a3e0-3b4821a83167" :question/math-academy-id "q-281499" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a5390c0531968e8ef7f3817aa7ceb539ea08633fbfce611736cd3b548c17c1e.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the given curve is $r=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a circle of radius $|a|=|-1.5|=1.5$ centered at the point $(0,-1.5).$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the equation of such a circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2a\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1.5)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ad4cf0e-4346-5277-9026-122b5ae58f25" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given a Point on the Circumference"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bf6bafc-4476-5a9e-af7f-e7e2638c73cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bf6bafc-4476-5a9e-af7f-e7e2638c73cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542756} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cf19871-6dd2-593d-bf3f-f7ad27ac4fd0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e8e220e-5d02-535f-8203-20819203c421" :question/math-academy-id "q-281737" :question/problem "If the polar curve $r=k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(-3,-3),$  then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(3\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =k(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\ k & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/72/72cb94d6ed11f7502e7810d7d201ba71e594ed97a7a3eadd789732ca3dcb8968.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ad4cf0e-4346-5277-9026-122b5ae58f25" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given a Point on the Circumference"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5351d84c-4882-5054-a0c1-cbc0e962e2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5351d84c-4882-5054-a0c1-cbc0e962e2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542864} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bbb16c8-6341-55ff-b300-2282e814f399" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0054ec1e-580f-564e-8493-248291e1368b" :question/math-academy-id "q-281742" :question/problem "If the polar curve $r=k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(-3,\\sqrt{3}),$  then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & =k(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/def95f29f6397b0f6eab20afb6327d8f3f99fdc52fbeac0b0c2af8b228d060a9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3504bc1-05d2-55c5-b862-84be2e2516f7" :knowledge-point/title "The Polar Equation of a Circle Centered on the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61e8d881-ba1f-5ceb-b3f3-5e65608dd0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/11c04b13e2182d2ea29f2b880f1ec98adce669ece3fda27cbcab3f62bfe468c1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542906} {:answer/id #uuid "759a332a-aacd-5a9b-a528-693432d5769d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d102979e445cad0a12f5eadde35664b21fe298faa7180285f430ad7fffe8ca59.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542950} {:answer/id #uuid "7bac5fbb-2490-53e6-92c7-b61639d48653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1e/1ef95d469be1e5c6a327feb54bf2a5716ea134b06efa49007c32ebb1134915b8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542969} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f10563-2c3f-5c45-8200-4edcd650eda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/11/110736084be735e5e103fe6c541cc3f98140743495cc7d23cbb2242c0eade076.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543221} {:answer/id #uuid "ed58c891-ee8c-5063-a091-2e3c6626f6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a9/a918435617bd32269f0b53a11adcbebd420a21d4e3661ff35457